        - A Central Exhaust Unit (Zephyr JR @ 160 CFM) - that co-vents the bathroom &amp; kitchen/living room areas
             - Exhausts out the lower back wall of your house
             - This fan appears to be wired into your furnace so that it operates 100% of the time the furnace is on
        - A 5" fresh air supply pipe that comes up from below the house directly into the mechanical room and sealed
          to the rear of the return air ductwork of the furnace
             - This fresh air supply will bring in outside fresh air anytime one of the other exhaust fans outlined above is
               operating &amp; whenever the furnace fan is operating
             - There is usually an inline damper on this 5" fresh air pipe that will allow you to manually adjust the amount of
                fresh air the house receives - however I did not see one on yours (perhaps it's there &amp; I just missed it ?)

I mentioned that I have evaluated many houses similar to yours and located in various NWT Communities. I have seen your style of house that includes the Zephyr JR central ventilation system and some newer versions of this house that do not have it at all (they have a bathroom fan that operates independently and vents directly out of the house). In fact, I evaluated one of each style in Fort McPherson while I was there in February !

For the houses that have the Zephyr JR central ventilation system, I have often seen it interconnected to the furnace fan, but usually through something called a "summer switch" located inside the furnace room on the right side wall. I have been told the purpose of the "summer switch" is to provide additional ventilation &amp; cooling during the non-heating season by allowing the Zephyr JR fan to vent warmer air out of the house (from the 2 x high wall vent openings in the bathroom and in the kitchen/living room areas) to the outside, and the furnace fan would pull in outside (and cooler) fresh air from below the house (through the 5" fresh air supply pipe) and evenly distribute it throughout the house via the furnace ductwork.

It is my understanding that, during the heating season, the Zephyr JR exhaust vent should only be operating whenever the switch in the bathroom or kitchen is turned on. In your house, the Zephyr JR exhaust vent system seems to operate each time the furnace is on &amp; heating the house (a test I completed together with your son while I was at your house). If your furnace is on for 30 minutes out of each hour, it will be using the Zephyr JR fan (at 160 CFM) to vent 4,800 cubic feet of warm heated air to the outside of your house each hour ! My calculations indicate that your house has about 9,600 cubic feet of interior heated air.

As the Zephyr JR exhaust fan is now quite old, and I see newer versions of your house design that no longer have it ......... perhaps it may be a good idea to replace it with just a bathroom fan that vents out the lower back wall (where the Zephyr unit currently goes out). In my opinion, the new bathroom fan can be installed in the upper wall of the bathroom (where the current vent hole it located) and wired only to the switch in the bathroom. The wiring that connects to the switch in the kitchen could be used to locate a humidistat switch in the central hallway (right through the wall from where the current kitchen switch is located) and this humidistat switch could be used in summer to operate the bathroom fan more frequently and operate the furnace fan during summer to help ventilate the house.

If you install a new bathroom fan, please choose an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units, and include a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so the bath fan will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

Savings:
By replacing the 160 CFM Zephyr JR fan with perhaps a 70 CFM ENERGY STARÂ® bathroom fan, you could potentially save $ 25 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ. These savings will be significantly under-valued if the current Zephyr JR fan is no longer used to vent warm air out of the house each time the furnace is operating during the heating season !!

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort McPherson, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, clothes dryer, and Zephyr JR central exhaust), indicated a pressure drop of 8.6 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could be expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace and hot water tank are chimney vented and can be potential risks for having combustion products back-drafted into the house. See my comments in the Air Sealing section of this report about possibly weather-stripping the dual doors of the central mechanical room ........... reducing the risk of potential back-drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, but did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort McPherson

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort McPherson:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.69 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.73 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $14.00 per bag (picked up in Inuvik), $750.00 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

